From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: a pointer crosses monitors, and the keys go with it

A desk of several monitors is a browser window per CRTC, and the
pointer could not leave the one it started on. Upstream `DrmCursor`
clamps the cursor into the window it is on; moving it to the next
display is ash's `ExtendedMouseWarpController`, and a views browser has
no ash.

So `DrmCursor::MoveCursor` asks `PointerCrossingFor` where a move that
left its window lands, and carries the cursor there: hidden on the old
CRTC, shown on the new one, exactly as `MoveCursorTo(window, ...)` does
from the UI thread. The next located event is at a screen point inside
the new window, so `CanDispatchEvent` routes it there with no further
change. The windows are kept on the cursor, under its lock, because a
hand's motion arrives on the evdev thread and the window manager is the
UI thread's.

Across the row, and only to a screen that touches it: the compositor
steps connectors across one row in the order a profile places them, and
puts the dark ones a pixel past the end so a pointer cannot wander onto
a panel that is off. By the upright edge rather than the panel's: a
monitor stood on its side is still a landscape CRTC, so the edge and the
height are read turned, with the rotation `FollowDisplayTurn` already
hands `CursorController`.

AND THE KEYS. Every `DrmWindowHost` answered yes to every key event --
"For non-ash builds we would need smarter keyboard focus" -- so the
first window added took every key, and a page on any other monitor could
put up a focused box it would never hear a key in. The keyboard is now
on the monitor the pointer is on (`HasTheKeyboard`), which is the one
thing a page and the engine agree about without a shell saying so.

Nothing here is seen by a shell: it is the pointer and the keys arriving
at the page on the screen they are on.
